; NextDAAD XBN extern support Task 1 fixture - EXTERN/CALL/#int probe.
;
; Compiled by tests\build-tests.ps1 on every run; staged as the active
; GAME.DDB in sd\XBN\ by tests\build-tests.ps1 -Xbn, which also stages
; the fixture extern binary tests\xbn\xbntest.asm assembles to
; tests\out\xbn\GAME.XBN as GAME.XBN in that same folder (-XbnNoBin
; omits it entirely for the XABS/no-XBN control; -XbnBad <kind> stages
; a corrupt-header/truncated variant instead, for Task 2). Skeleton
; (CTL/VOC/STX/MTX/OTX/LTX/CON/OBJ/PRO shape, required system message
; block) follows tests\gmodegate.dsf.
;
; WHAT IT PINS
; Task 1 is scaffolding only - these verbs assert the EXTERN/CALL/#int
; CONTRACT xbn.inc documents, not any interpreter behaviour: zero
; interpreter source changes land until Task 2+, so every one of these
; checks is EXPECTED TO FAIL (or no-op) until then.
;   XREG - the register contract EXTERN hands the extern on entry:
;          A=B=param1, C=fn, HL=flags+param1, DE=objTable+param1*6,
;          IX=flags base ($A200). xbntest.asm's ext_main echoes each of
;          those into flags 200-205; this entry asserts five legs plus
;          the DE BASE - the HL leg against a seeded flag 42 (restored
;          after: it is FLAG_PROMPT), the DE base against object 0's
;          not-created start (252) via a second echo at param1=0. The
;          *6 stride itself is unproven (index 0 hides it).
;   XCAL - a direct CALL into a staged XBN reaches the intended
;          address (xbntest.asm's call_target, which sets flag 220).
;   XCNR - negative control: CALL to an address outside the mapped XBN
;          window ($8000, well below XBN_ORG) must no-op, not crash.
;   XTIK - the #int hook (xbntest.asm's int_tick) increments a flag
;          every frame while an XBN is staged.
;   XSVC - the service probe entry (fn 21); Task 6 fills flags 206+
;          once the service jump table exists.
;   XABS - the no-XBN control: with NO GAME.XBN staged (-XbnNoBin),
;          EXTERN must stay inert and flag 200 must stay at the CLEARed
;          0, never the 9 a staged XBN would echo.
;   XFIO - the file-service probe (fn 22, xbntest.asm's fio_probe):
;          SVC_FOPEN/FWRITE/FCLOSE then SVC_FOPEN/FREAD/FCLOSE round-trip
;          8 bytes through XBNFIO.TMP on the card and compares them;
;          CLEARs flag 208 first.
;   XMSG - the GETMSG probe (fn 23, xbntest.asm's msg_probe): decodes
;          MTX message 0 into savStage, echoes its first byte and
;          length, then re-prints it through a bounded SVC_PUTCHAR
;          loop; asserts the decoded length (flag 209 = 25); fn 24
;          (msg_probe_bad) then checks the out-of-range error path
;          (message 255 - out of range while the MTX holds fewer than
;          256 messages; MES literals compile into the MTX, so a small
;          probe number rots into range).
;   XCND - EXTERN condition semantics (fn 26, xbntest.asm's condition
;          probe: scf/ret, ALWAYS fails). Pins the split return path:
;          a forwarded extern's CF-set verdict fails the entry AND
;          clears the done state. First entry is the ruled edge - a
;          MES runs (stamping done) before the failing EXTERN, and the
;          clear must win anyway.
;   XCNC - EXTERN condition semantics, control (fn 27, xbntest.asm's
;          or a/ret: ALWAYS succeeds). The entry must complete.
;   XFRM - SVC_FRAMES (fn 28 snapshot, fn 29 delta, xbntest.asm's
;          frames_snap/frames_delta): asserts the counter advances at
;          the frame rate across a 50-frame PAUSE.
;   XDAT - SVC_GETDATE presence probe (fn 36, xbntest.asm's
;          getdate_probe): flag 223 poisoned to 9 by LET, must land 0
;          or 1 (never 9) - RTC presence varies by fitting/emulator, so
;          either outcome passes; only the poison surviving is a fail.
;   XRSK - SVC_RANDOM hardened (Task 5): arms the int_tick soak (fn 35,
;          xbntest.asm's rng_soak_arm) so the #int hook draws
;          SVC_RANDOM once per tick, WITH INTERRUPTS DISABLED (the case
;          the svc_random DI/EI bracket exists for), while the mainline
;          sits in a PAUSE. The sticky bit (flag 219) must read 1: a
;          wedged or torn shared stream would leave it 0 forever. Also
;          proves no deadlock from the DI bracket - a second arm/disarm
;          cycle after this one must still complete. Foreground-
;          concurrent CHANCE stress against the same interleave is
;          covered by ordinary suite play; this verb isolates the
;          hook-draw/atomicity claim on its own.
;   XBSY - SVC_BUSY (Task 7, fn 37, xbntest.asm's busy_probe): the
;          foreground-invisibility contract itself - every busy source
;          (vidPlaying, cardBusy, palBusy) is synchronous with the
;          mainline, so a foreground read is ALWAYS 0. Flag 228 arrives
;          poisoned to 9 (LET), must never keep that value. Hook-side
;          visibility of bits 0/2 is silicon/RC territory (video
;          emulator ban); bit 1 hook-capture likewise rides the RC sheet.
;   XPAL - SVC_PALREAD (Task 8, fn 38, xbntest.asm's palread_probe):
;          draws picture 1, reads all 256 palette entries back and sums
;          them; the 16-bit checksum (flags 229/230) is pinned against
;          the picture's own embedded palette plus the interpreter's
;          documented transforms - see the XPAL entry below for the
;          derivation.
;   XWIN - SVC_WINDOW (Task 9, fn 39, xbntest.asm's window_probe):
;          selects window 2, prints a marker through it, restores the
;          previous window (flag 231) - proves the switch actually
;          happened rather than a stub returning a plausible number.
;
; MTX message 0 is reserved verbatim as the Task 8 SVC_GETMSG probe
; payload ("TICKER MESSAGE FOR GETMSG") - never printed as a boot
; banner, so a later task can read it back through the service call
; and compare bytes rather than screen text. Task 9's shipped ticker
; worked example (authoring-kit\externs\ticker\ticker.asm) consumes
; the same message 0 through the same service call.
;   XTCK - drives the Task 9 ticker example (EXTERN 0 30 arms it on
;          message 0, EXTERN 0 31 disarms it). This verb does not
;          reach xbntest.asm at all - -XbnTicker (tests\build-tests.ps1)
;          stages the ticker's own GAME.XBN in place of the fixture, so
;          this entry is only meaningful in that combination; against
;          the plain fixture XBN, fn 30/31 fall through xbntest.asm's
;          ext_main unrecognised and no-op.
;   XSLT - CALLs pinned jump table slots 0, 1, 2 and 3 (toolkit fns,
;          via flag 249), then slot 4 - unowned, must print nothing.
;          Flag 249 is MOVED mid-verb before slot 2's call, so a
;          dispatch hardcoded to one flag number rather than actually
;          reading 249 cannot fake the result - this is the only way a
;          CALL, which carries no parameter, proves the indirection at
;          all. Also proves the reserved-slot no-op guarantee still
;          holds now that slots 0-3 are occupied. Meaningful only on
;          legs staging a table-carrying binary (-XbnAll/-XbnTicker/
;          -XbnFade/-XbnClock/-XbnTool) - the fixture binary carries no
;          such table.
;
; XTKP/XTKA/XTKO/XTKT drive the Task 6 toolkit module (fns 70-75, 82, 83),
; part of the same -XbnTool combined binary as -XbnClock/-XbnHints. Each
; flag number in these verbs is a FLAG NUMBER, not a value - the toolkit
; fns read/write flags at $A200+n, LOW BYTE FIRST for a pair.
;   XTKP - decimal printing (fn 70/71) and the width flag (248): 42
;          unpadded, padded to five columns with spaces, then zeros,
;          then a 16-bit pair. The high byte is left non-zero (flag
;          101 = 7) through the three fn 70 (byte print) calls, so a
;          byte/pair confusion in fn 70 would show.
;   XTKA - 16-bit arithmetic (fn 75/73/74): pair += the pair at flag
;          249, pair -= immediate byte, then a compare against all
;          three outcomes - greater (2), equal (1), less (0) into flag
;          251, so a hardcoded "return greater" cannot pass.
;   XTKO - overflow (fn 72): 65530 + 10 wraps to 4 and sets flag 251,
;          THEN a second, non-overflowing add proves flag 251 also
;          goes back to 0 - otherwise a module that always sets the
;          overflow flag would pass unnoticed.
;   XTKT - time formats (fn 82/83): two byte flags as HH:MM, then two
;          16-bit second counts as MM:SS, the second chosen to exercise
;          zero-padding on the SECONDS digit (01:05), then a re-print
;          of the HH:MM with flag 248 set to a zero-pad width, which
;          the time formats must IGNORE.
;
; XTKI/XTKS exercise the status-line pattern a real game uses: the toolkit
; cannot select a window itself, so the author brackets the call with
; WINDOW and the print path handles width, colour and wrapping - no other
; verb here does that. Hardware-only, typed by hand, not asserted against
; any expected output.
;   XTKI - arms live values: score 800, clock running from 12:00 at 1
;          in-game minute/real second, a 5-minute real-seconds timer.
;   XTKS - paints WINDOW 2 from those values (score width 5, HH:MM,
;          MM:SS) and restores WINDOW 1. Repaintable on demand.
;
; -XbnHints and -XbnAll stage a packed GAME.HNT alongside the combined
; binary, for the hints worked example (authoring-kit\externs\hints).
; XHNP/XHNQ/XHNA/XHNR/XHNT drive it, all on TOPIC 0 - the shipped HINTS.TXT gives
; topic 0 exactly two levels and topic 1 exactly three, and MODE_R (the
; file-open read mode) is also 1, so a build that lost the topic operand
; would still answer correctly for topic 1 and hide the bug. XHNT alone
; drives topic 1, to prove the operand actually reaches the index: if it
; ever reports the same count as XHNQ, the topic parameter is not
; getting through.
;   XHNP - preflight (fn 52): flag 243 poisoned to 9 first, must come back 0
;   XHNQ - level count (fn 51) for topic 0: flag 243 must read 2.
;   XHNA - automatic mode (fn 50), three calls. "Two DIFFERENT texts
;          then status 3" holds only against a VIRGIN GAME.HPR (topic 0
;          at level 0) - run right after XHNR, or on a fresh card.
;   XHNR - clear progress (fn 53), then one automatic call must print
;          topic 0's FIRST hint again, proving the reset took; prints
;          flag 243 after the reset (expect reset 0).
;   XHNT - level count (fn 51) for topic 1: flag 243 must read 3.
;   XHNL - explicit-level mode (flag 242): pinned level prints with
;          status 0, one past the last level reports 3; GAME.HPR is
;          never touched, so this is safe to type at any point.
;
; XCLK/XCLA/XCLS drive the Task 4 clock module (fn 60/61/62) and XTMR/XTMI/
; XTLR drive the timer module (fn 63-65) - both part of the same -XbnClock
; combined binary as -XbnHints. RUN THEM IN ORDER: XCLS is order-dependent -
; it stops the clock and compares two readings of flag 225 for equality,
; which holds vacuously if the clock was never armed and running, so it only
; proves anything typed AFTER XCLK. By the time XTMI runs, XCLS has already
; stopped the clock AND fn 60 (EXTERN 0 60) is a documented no-op once armed
; (clock.asm's run: tests its own armed byte, not flag 226), so the clock is
; FROZEN for XTMI - it does not rely on the clock running, it drives it
; explicitly with fn 62 (advance), which works whether or not flag 226 is
; set. XCDY drives the day counter (fn 62) across a midnight crossing.
;   XCLK - arm and run the clock at 1 in-game minute per real second, from
;          12:00. PAUSE then PRINT: minutes must have advanced.
;   XCLA - advance 90 in-game minutes from 12:00: must read 13:30, the hour
;          carry.
;   XCLS - stop must freeze the clock (type AFTER XCLK - see above).
;   XTMR - a 2-second timer must expire. State 3 = expired.
;   XTMI - the cross-module check: arms a deadline against a clock frozen
;          at 720 (12:00), prints state 2, then advances the clock PAST the
;          deadline with fn 62 and prints state again - must read 3.
;   XTLR - a RAMLOAD under a running minute timer. Clobbers the deadline
;          pair itself (not just the clock) between the RAMSAVE and the
;          RAMLOAD, so the restore has real work to do.
;   XTMS - fn 64 stops all three timer slots: states clear, pairs stay.
;   XCDY - the day counter must advance by exactly one across a midnight
;          crossing.
;
; SAVE/LOAD/XCHK/XSVT/XSVD/XADV/XAD1/XRUN/XSVE extend clock/timer coverage
; to a REAL SAVE and LOAD - the file-backed condacts, which XTLR's
; RAMSAVE/RAMLOAD never touch the SD card and cannot exercise. XSVT/XSVD/
; XSVE arm a known state and print it with XCHK's line. The hand protocol
; MUST mutate between the SAVE and the LOAD or it proves nothing:
; RESTART does not clear flags, so with no mutation all three readings
; match whether or not LOAD restored anything. Type:
;   XCHK, SAVE, XADV (or XAD1), XCHK, LOAD, XCHK
; and require reading 2 to DIFFER from reading 1 (the mutation took) and
; reading 3 to MATCH reading 1 (the load undid it).
;
; The clock is left STOPPED by every setup verb (XSVT/XSVD/XSVE) - running
; it left every reading dependent on how long the tester took to type the
; next command, so nothing but slot 1's real-time pair could be compared
; across a save/load. XRUN starts it, for one check only: proving the
; clock still ticks after a load. Time otherwise moves only on purpose,
; via XADV/XAD1 (fn 62), which works whether or not the clock is running.
;
; Every verb here that changes clock/timer state and then prints inserts
; PAUSE 4 (2 frames, DRC halves PAUSE) before the print block: state
; changes are immediate, but a timer's expiry is decided by the 50Hz
; frame hook, which can lag up to 20ms behind the verb that armed it, so
; a print straight after the change can catch the timer still counting
; down when it is about to expire. XCHK changes nothing and stays a pure
; probe with no PAUSE.
;
;   SAVE - SAVE 0 then RESTART (condition-typed - RESTART only runs on
;          success).
;   LOAD - LOAD 0 then RESTART, same shape.
;   XCHK - readback probe: clock and timer state on one line, nothing
;          else, for before/after comparison. No PAUSE.
;   XSVT - arms the standard save-test state: clock STOPPED at 12:00,
;          rate 250 (5 real seconds/in-game minute), slot 0 a 90-minute
;          deadline, slot 1 a 300-second control. PAUSE 4, XCHK's line.
;   XSVD - two minutes short of midnight (23:58) with a 2-minute
;          duration, so one XAD1 step lands exactly on the boundary.
;          Clock STOPPED, rate 250. PAUSE 4, XCHK's line.
;   XADV - EXTERN 250 62: advances the clock 250 in-game minutes, PAUSE
;          4, XCHK's line - moves the clock a long way between a SAVE
;          and a LOAD without a real-time wait.
;   XAD1 - EXTERN 1 62: advances the clock exactly one in-game minute,
;          PAUSE 4, XCHK's line - steps a deadline boundary one minute
;          at a time.
;   XRUN - LET 226 1, PAUSE 4, XCHK's line - starts the clock at
;          whatever rate is set. Proves the clock still ticks after a
;          load; nothing else here starts it.
;   XSVE - the sharp expiry-boundary setup: XSVT's shape with a 2-minute
;          deadline, so two XAD1 steps walk it across expiry. Clock
;          STOPPED at 12:00, rate 250. PAUSE 4, XCHK's line.
;
;
; LEG SCOPE - which staging leg each verb is meaningful on. A verb typed
; on the wrong leg prints plausible zeros and proves nothing - the H2
; lesson: a run-sheet check once drove fn 22 against a binary with no
; fn 22 handler and its "pass" was void.
;   fixture leg (plain -Xbn, stages xbntest.asm) ONLY - the one leg
;     where fns 20-29 and 35-39 exist: XREG XCAL XTIK XSVC XFIO
;     XMSG XMS6 XCND XCNC XFRM XRSK XDAT XBSY XPAL XWIN (fns 26-29 and
;     35-39 exist only in xbntest.asm) (on any other leg these print
;     unwritten flags - zeros)
;   XDAT lives in the fixture leg's fn table (fn 36) but is
;     SILICON-ONLY: DO NOT TYPE it in an emulator replay - ZEsarUX's
;     esxdos handler lacks M_GETDATE and the call derails the machine.
;     Runs only in a real-hardware RC silicon session.
;   no-XBN leg (-XbnNoBin): XABS expects 0; on the fixture leg it
;     expects 9. Any other leg: meaningless.
;   COMBINED-binary legs - -XbnAll, -XbnHints, -XbnClock and -XbnTool
;     all stage ALL.XBN, so EVERY collection verb is meaningful there:
;     XTCK XFAD XFDI XFSC XFSO XDRK XHNP XHNQ XHNA XHNR XHNT XHNL
;     XCLK XCLA XCLS XTMR XTMI XTLR XTMS XCDY XSVT XSVD XADV XAD1 XRUN
;     XSVE XCHK XTKP XTKA XTKO XTKT XTKI XTKS XSLT
;     EXCEPT the hints verbs (XHNP XHNQ XHNA XHNR XHNT XHNL), which also
;     need a packed GAME.HNT - staged only by -XbnAll and -XbnHints. On
;     -XbnClock/-XbnTool they report file-missing (243 = 1; fn 51 gives
;     0): a leg artefact, not a module fault.
;   -XbnTicker stages the STANDALONE ticker example: only XTCK is
;     meaningful there (plus XSLT with all slots printing nothing).
;   -XbnFade stages the STANDALONE fade example: only XFAD XFDI XFSC
;     XFSO XDRK are meaningful there (plus the no-op XSLT form).
;   XSLT prints toolkit output only on toolkit-carrying legs; on other
;     pinned-table legs every slot must print nothing. NEVER type it on
;     the fixture leg - no call table there, $C00E is fixture code.
;   any leg with art staged: XGFB XRST XRLD XDCL XARM (interpreter-side
;     GFX/PICTURE machinery; no extern involvement)
;   any leg: XCNR SAVE LOAD

